ic situations there is actually a  body language of power and we know it  but we know it so well that we don't  know that we know it's something that  we've been learning through our  socialization since a very very young  age  so we learn how to tune our bodies to  specific situations and specific  relationships when we need to show  someone respect or be deferential we  know how to use our body in a way that  allows us to be effective in that role  and we learn how to take charge with our  bodies and how to make sure that other  people aren't taking advantage of us  most of us are socialized you know over  the course of our lives based on other  people's expectations of where they  think we should be within a hierarchy  and so we learn how to do certain  aspects of this sort of performance very  well in other parts we know less well  and sometimes we find ourselves in  situations where the things we do with  our bodies that come most naturally  don't fit the situation at all  so I want to raise awareness of the ways  in which we use our bodies and the  impact that it has on our ability to  have power and influence if you want to  be strategic about acting with power  there are two objectives that you have  to keep in mind the first one is that  you want to be able to show up  authoritative in charge able to make the  decisions and able to privilege your  knowledge and experience over the  knowledge and experience of other people  the same time you need to be able to  show approachable which is open  empathetic willing to take others  knowledge and experiences into account  and be able to relate to people on a  human level so let me see if I can start  by giving you a framework for thinking  about two main objectives for addressing  a set of questions about how your body  language can be used to change your  impact there are really two things I  think you need to be able to do if you  want to have influence the first is you  need to be able to show up authoritative